Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Period live-action London, 1843. On Christmas Eve, fog presses the windows. EBENEZER SCROOGE, a spare old man with a pointed nose, frosted white hair, wiry chin, blue lips, and a severe black waistcoat, counts coins beside a miserly fire. BOB CRATCHIT, thin and pale in a threadbare brown coat and long white comforter, copies letters in the cold outer office.

(SOUND: quill scratching, coal settling, muffled church bells in fog)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. Scrooge and Marley was the firm, and Scrooge never painted out Old Marley's name.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy-faced, bright-eyed nephew in a green wool coat, bursts in from the frost.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely.

BOB involuntarily applauds, then hurriedly pokes his dying fire.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Come and dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED exits, smiling sadly at BOB.

MR BRADLEY, a portly charity collector in a dark frock coat, enters with a ledger and removes his hat.

MR BRADLEY
At this festive season, we seek some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR BRADLEY
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR BRADLEY
They are.

SCROOGE
Then I wish to be left alone. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR BRADLEY closes his ledger and withdraws.

SCROOGE
You will want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
Be here all the earlier next morning.

EXT. LONDON STREET - NIGHT

Fog swallows gas lamps. SCROOGE passes holly-bright shop windows, a brazier crowded by ragged families, and warm rooms where strangers greet one another.

(SOUND: wind through alleyways, distant carols, boots crunching ice)

SCROOGE turns his collar up and walks alone into the dark.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

At a black old gateway, SCROOGE fits his key to a large iron knocker. The knocker becomes the livid face of MARLEY: ghostly spectacles on his brow, fixed eyes, and hair stirred by an unseen breath.

(SOUND: a low metallic groan beneath the wind)

SCROOGE blinks. The face is only a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE sits in his dressing-gown beside a low fire, a bowl of gruel at hand. A disused bell over his chair begins to sway.

(SOUND: one faint bell ring growing into a violent peal)

Every bell in the house rings. From the cellar below comes the drag of an immense chain.

(SOUND: iron chains clanking up the stairs, floorboards shuddering)

The door opens without a hand. MARLEY enters: transparent, in his old pigtail, waistcoat, tights, and boots. A chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and purses coils around him.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who were you?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you!

MARLEY raises a terrible cry. His bandage slips; his jaw drops upon his breast.

(SOUND: MARLEY'S chain crashes across the floor)

SCROOGE falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business.

MARLEY points at SCROOGE.

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

MARLEY backs toward the window. It rises by itself, revealing chained phantoms wheeling through the night sky.

(SOUND: sorrowful wails and the rush of bitter wind)

MARLEY
Look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

MARLEY floats out. SCROOGE stares at the double-locked door, then collapses into bed without undressing.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

SCROOGE lies awake under frost-rimmed curtains. His repeater, the church clock, and every chime agree upon midnight, then one.

(SOUND: a deep, hollow clock striking ONE)

White light floods the room. The curtains draw apart to reveal CHRISTMAS PAST, a child-sized figure with an unwrinkled face, white hair, a white tunic, bare feet, holly and summer flowers, and a clear flame shining from its crown.

(SOUND: a bright, trembling chord)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

CHRISTMAS PAST grips SCROOGE'S arm.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation. Rise, and walk with me.

INT. BOARDING SCHOOLROOM - DAY

Snow whitens the broken panes of a bare schoolroom. YOUNG SCROOGE, a thin dark-haired boy in a plain black school coat, reads alone by a feeble fire while unseen adult SCROOGE and CHRISTMAS PAST watch.

(SOUND: mice behind paneling, a drip from the gutter, pages turning)

CHRISTMAS PAST
A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

SCROOGE watches his younger self laugh and cry over imaginary heroes in the firelight.

SCROOGE
Poor boy.

The schoolroom door opens. FAN, a small fair-haired girl in a blue wool cape, runs to YOUNG SCROOGE and throws her arms around him.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever.

SCROOGE
Fan.

CHRISTMAS PAST
She had a large heart. She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

A younger SCROOGE, now a slim apprentice in a brown waistcoat, and DICK WILKINS race to close the shutters. FEZZIWIG, round, rosy, and white-wigged, beams from his high desk.

(SOUND: shutters banging closed, fiddle tuning, delighted laughter)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

The warehouse transforms into a warm ballroom. Employees whirl in a country dance while FEZZIWIG and his wife lead the floor.

(MUSIC: a lively fiddle playing “Sir Roger de Coverley”)

CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small! He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil.

SCROOGE watches YOUNG SCROOGE clap with the dancers. His pleasure fades into regret.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOR - NIGHT

A modest parlor glows around a winter fire. BELLE, a gentle young woman in a mourning dress, faces YOUNG SCROOGE, whose eager eyes have become hard with care.

(SOUND: fire ticking, a clock measuring the silence)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, I have no just cause to grieve.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing on which the world is so hard as poverty.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. The master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

YOUNG SCROOGE cannot answer.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

BELLE turns away.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

SCROOGE reaches toward her shadow.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home!

CHRISTMAS PAST'S head-light burns fiercely. SCROOGE seizes its extinguisher-cap and forces it down.

(SOUND: a rising hiss of light, abruptly muffled)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The room has become a Christmas grove. Holly, ivy, geese, turkeys, puddings, fruit, and steaming punch surround a roaring hearth. CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits enthroned among the food: a broad giant in a green fur-trimmed robe, dark brown curls, holly crown, bare feet, and an open, genial face.

(SOUND: fire roaring, punch bubbling, distant joyful bells)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON MARKET STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

Snow is swept from doorsteps. Bakers, grocers, and poulterers glow with food and light as families hurry past carrying covered dinners.

(SOUND: shovel blades scraping snow, market cries, basket handles knocking)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT lifts his torch and sprinkles light over a poor family's small pudding. Their quarrel softens into smiles.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE takes the green robe and follows.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

In four cramped rooms, MRS CRATCHIT, brisk and proud in a twice-turned blue gown with bright ribbons, prepares the table. BOB, still thin in his threadbare coat and long white comforter, enters carrying TINY TIM, a frail boy with brown curls, a little crutch, and iron supports on his legs.

(SOUND: potatoes rattling under a saucepan lid, a kettle singing)

BOB settles TINY TIM on a stool by the fire.

BOB
He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family sets a goose before them. MRS CRATCHIT brings in a flaming pudding crowned with holly.

(SOUND: a burst of cheering, brandy flames crackling)

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

(MUSIC: a soft, hopeful hymn beneath the crackling fire)

SCROOGE studies TINY TIM'S crutch.

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

SCROOGE bows his head, struck by his own words.

MRS CRATCHIT
It should be Christmas Day, I am sure, on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

BOB
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

FRED, still ruddy-faced and bright-eyed in his green coat, laughs beside a laden dinner table. His dimpled, dark-haired wife, MRS FRED, sits near the hearth while friends play games around them. Invisible SCROOGE smiles among them.

(SOUND: warm laughter, glasses clinking, chairs scraping during a game)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

MRS FRED
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

FRED lifts a glass.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not. Uncle Scrooge!

The company raises their glasses.

(MUSIC: a harp plays a simple, tender air)

SCROOGE tries to answer, but no one can hear him.

EXT. LONDON SQUARE - MIDNIGHT

CHRISTMAS PRESENT stands worn and grey beneath a clock tower. From the folds of his green robe crawl two ragged children: IGNORANCE, a yellow, scowling boy, and WANT, a gaunt, frightened girl.

(SOUND: wind whipping through the square, a clock tolling midnight)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

(SOUND: the final stroke of twelve fading into a low funeral drone)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT vanishes. Through the mist comes the FUTURE SPIRIT, tall and stately beneath a deep black hood, its face wholly hidden and one hand extended.

EXT. ROYAL EXCHANGE - DAY

The FUTURE SPIRIT glides past merchants beneath the cold stone arches. Men in black coats gather, laugh briefly, and disperse with no grief among them.

(SOUND: coins chinking in pockets, carriage wheels rattling over cobbles)

One merchant yawns. Another mimics a coffin lid closing. SCROOGE searches the crowd for his own accustomed place and finds another man seated there.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. Lead on.

The black hand points toward the city slums.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Rusty keys, chains, rags, bottles, and scales fill a low, smoky shop. OLD JOE, a grey-haired dealer with a pipe, weighs stolen goods while HESTER, a sharp-eyed charwoman in a patched shawl, and MRS DILBER, a laundress in a faded bonnet, unload silver, sheets, and heavy bed-curtains.

(SOUND: scale pans clinking, a charcoal stove hissing)

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

HESTER
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

OLD JOE counts coins onto the floor.

(SOUND: coins rolling across bare boards, OLD JOE'S dry laugh)

HESTER
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

SCROOGE recoils from the glittering pile.

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare, uncurtained bed stands in a dark empty room. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a still body, plundered, unwatched, unwept, and uncared for. The FUTURE SPIRIT points at its hidden face.

(SOUND: a cat clawing at the door, rats gnawing beneath the hearthstone)

SCROOGE reaches toward the sheet but cannot lift it.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me.

(MUSIC: a low, mournful cello note)

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

The Cratchit home is quiet. MRS CRATCHIT, in the same blue gown now faded at the sleeves, sews with lowered eyes. PETER reads beside the hearth. BOB, thinner still, enters in his worn coat and long white comforter; TINY TIM'S stool and crutch stand empty.

(SOUND: a clock ticking loudly in the silence)

BOB looks toward the empty stool.

BOB
I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is.

BOB climbs the stairs. He sits beside a small bed and kisses the unseen little face.

(SOUND: BOB'S broken breath, a floorboard creaking overhead)

BOB
My little, little child!

MRS CRATCHIT and the children gather around BOB when he returns. They hold one another in silence.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Weeds knot around a neglected grave in a churchyard walled by houses. The FUTURE SPIRIT stands beside the stone, pointing down.

(SOUND: freezing wind through dead grass, a distant bell tolling)

SCROOGE crawls forward. Snow melts on the carved name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The black finger points from the grave to SCROOGE and back again.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

SCROOGE clutches the Spirit's robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

(SOUND: the wind rises into a fierce howl, then cuts off)

The FUTURE SPIRIT collapses into darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS MORNING

SCROOGE clings to his own bedpost. Sunlight pours through clear windows; his curtains hang whole around him.

(SOUND: church bells ringing in glorious peals)

SCROOGE
They are not torn down! I am here! The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. They will be. I know they will!

SCROOGE laughs, cries, and throws on his black coat inside out, then rights it.

(SOUND: SCROOGE'S great, unfamiliar laugh filling the room)

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy! A merry Christmas to everybody!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

SCROOGE leans out over the bright street in his best black coat. CHARLIE, a rosy-cheeked boy in Sunday clothes, looks up at him.

(SOUND: bells, cab wheels, cheerful voices, birds fluttering from eaves)

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

CHARLIE
To-day? Why, Christmas Day.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one?

CHARLIE
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
Go and buy the big prize Turkey. Tell them to bring it here.

A vast turkey soon rides away in a cab toward Camden Town. SCROOGE spots MR BRADLEY, the same portly charity collector in his dark frock coat, walking through the crowd.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! A merry Christmas to you!

MR BRADLEY
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE leans close and whispers an amount in MR BRADLEY'S ear.

MR BRADLEY
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

MR BRADLEY grips SCROOGE'S hands, overcome.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - NIGHT

FRED, still bright-eyed and ruddy, checks the festive table beside MRS FRED, whose dimpled face turns toward the door. SCROOGE opens it a crack and peers in.

(SOUND: serving dishes set down, guests laughing in the next room)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED rushes forward and embraces him.

FRED
Let him in? A merry Christmas, Uncle!

SCROOGE enters the warmth as the guests cheer.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

The next morning, SCROOGE, still spare and white-haired but bright-eyed in a neat black frock coat, waits beside a rebuilt fire. BOB, thin, pale, and wrapped in his long white comforter, slips in late and hurries toward his desk.

(SOUND: the office clock ticks nine, BOB'S wet boots squeak on the boards)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB approaches warily.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB stares, stunned.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

SCROOGE opens the coal-box and hands BOB the shovel.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

EXT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

A healthier TINY TIM, still carrying his little crutch but standing stronger in a green wool coat, walks between BOB and SCROOGE. MRS CRATCHIT watches from the doorway, smiling through tears.

(SOUND: children laughing in the snowy yard, a church bell warmly chiming)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

FADE OUT.
